= Discussion

#include "interpretation.typ"

This audit addresses the decision to update a specified remembered location from two RGB images. A robot could instead maintain a posterior over states, use an inventory prior, or request another view. Those options use additional information or a different decision objective. For the interface tested here, a physically correct guess on an exact pair still gives no evidence that the remembered location is empty. Reporting how often the system abstains on those pairs together with its clear-removal resolution exposes that distinction.

The main evaluation uses two chosen target meshes, two blocker families and one lighting condition per engine. The corridor follow-ups add two targets and one room family. The plain and furnished corridors reuse the same eight layouts. Multiple surfaces, lights and doorway features changed together, precluding attribution to a single room feature. The static removal intervention suppresses the target in the render. Changes that would follow from touching or displacing other objects require a dynamics experiment. The engines share the scene geometry and camera trajectories. The two renders therefore reveal sensitivity to rendering within the same design. Object additions, relocation, deformable occluders, physical cameras, calibrated real-world risk and end-to-end planning remain outside the observed evidence. The sample can show individual failures and count them on these panels. It does not isolate their causes. Sixteen main layouts give limited precision for differences between environments or object types.

The exact certificate is deliberately narrow. Different pixels can result from sampling, shadows, other optical effects, or informative target evidence. Distinguishing these effects requires evidence beyond a target silhouette. The calibration tests a local photometric cue at a fixed pose. Semantic support on partially occluded or moved-camera judgments remains unresolved. A subsequent active test could start from ambiguous pairs on fresh layouts and allow one additional view. Compare a selected viewpoint with fixed, random feasible and visibility-based choices at equal view and travel budgets. Include partly occluded targets and controls that remain hidden after the move. Count correctly resolved states and new false removals, keeping every unresolved pair in the denominator.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[Data and reproducibility]
The #link("https://github.com/Adithya-Rajendran/cmpe249-active-verification/releases/tag/scene-memory-audit-v1")[versioned data release] provides the exact images, raw decisions, software, and instructions for reproducing the comparison.
Version 1 archives the local protocol, target assignments, case and pair tables, calibration measurements, source and a second analysis implementation. The visibility analysis in this revision uses those archived decisions. The image archive contains every delivered RGB input with a content hash. Native outputs preserve masks and the recorded scene state for evaluator-side inspection. The study runs entirely in simulation.

#heading(level:2,numbering:none)[AI assistance]
Codex and Terra assisted with implementation, simulator operation, computational review and preparation of the manuscript. AI assistants checked both analysis implementations within the same project. The scorers received anonymous inputs with simulator state and masks withheld. Simulator interventions supplied physical-state labels. Pixel comparisons tested exact equality. Human validation of visual support and external replication remain open.
